## Proof

Let $\ln^{(j)} x$ denote the $j$-th iterated logarithm, defined recursively by $\ln^{(1)} x = \ln x$ and $\ln^{(j+1)} x = \ln(\ln^{(j)} x)$. Let $e_k$ denote the tower of exponentials of height $k$ ($e_1 = e$, $e_2 = e^e$, $e_3 = e^{e^e}$, …), so that $\ln^{(k)}(e_k) = 1$ and all iterated logarithms up to order $k$ are defined and positive on $[e_k, \infty)$.

**Key observation.** By the chain rule,
$$\frac{d}{dx}\ln^{(j)} x = \frac{1}{x\,\ln x\,\ln^{(2)} x \cdots \ln^{(j-1)} x}.$$

**Substitution.** Set $u = \ln^{(k)} x$. Then
$$du = \frac{dx}{x\,\ln x\,\ln^{(2)} x \cdots \ln^{(k-1)} x}.$$

The integrand rewrites as
$$\frac{dx}{x\,\ln x\,\ln^{(2)} x \cdots \ln^{(k-1)} x \cdot (\ln^{(k)} x)^s} = \frac{du}{u^s}.$$

**Change of limits.** When $x = e_k$, we have $u = \ln^{(k)}(e_k) = 1$. As $x \to \infty$, since $\ln^{(k)} x \to \infty$, we have $u \to \infty$. Therefore
$$\int_{e_k}^{\infty} \frac{dx}{x\,\ln x\,\ln^{(2)} x \cdots \ln^{(k-1)} x \cdot (\ln^{(k)} x)^s} = \int_{1}^{\infty} \frac{du}{u^s}.$$

**Convergence of the transformed integral.** The integral $\int_1^{\infty} u^{-s}\,du$ is the standard $p$-integral (in the variable $u$):
- If $s > 1$: $\int_1^{\infty} u^{-s}\,du = \left[\frac{u^{1-s}}{1-s}\right]_1^{\infty} = \frac{1}{s-1}$, which converges.
- If $s \le 1$: $\int_1^{\infty} u^{-s}\,du$ diverges (logarithmically when $s = 1$, polynomially when $s < 1$).

**Conclusion.** The original integral converges if and only if $s > 1$.

